WEBVTT

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:08.390
Nothing is off-limits when it comes to NASA getting the most out of all of its resources.

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:15.090
In fact, even a bit of perspiration is turned into inspiration by some of NASA's best and brightest.

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:18.870
NASA's version of sweat equity is next on Real World.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.980
Life on the International Space Station is a...

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:35.940
bit of a miracle. In the upper atmosphere, where the shuttle orbits, many of the elements

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:43.170
needed for human survival don't exist. So NASA has come up with some very creative ways

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:49.010
to ensure not only survival, but comfort as well. And they do it with something called

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:53.030
Environmental Control and Life Support Systems, or ECLSS.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:59.360
Basically ECLSSS encompasses any system that will help you live.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.340
without thinking.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.420
Moncie Roman is the project manager for

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:05.740
Exploration Life Support.

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:08.650
Basically clean air, clean water,

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:10.990
temperatures, humidities that are comfortable,

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:13.090
anything that will allow you to

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:14.870
survive in an area and

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:15.870
work comfortably.

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:19.390
ECLSS takes care of the air, removing

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:21.550
CO2 and other chemicals that are harmful

00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:23.710
to astronauts. It also provides

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:26.500
oxygen. ECLSS also

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.750
regulates humidity. Humidity

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:29.970
is moisture in the air.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:30.560
atmosphere.

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:36.210
We calculate relative humidity to get a sense of how much moisture is in the air.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:41.670
Relative humidity is a measure of the amount of water in the air compared with the amount

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:45.830
of water the air can hold at the temperature it happens to be when you are measuring it.

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:52.070
Relative humidity is expressed as a percentage and can be calculated by using this equation.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:59.300
Relative humidity equals actual vapor density divided by saturated vapor density times 100.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:06.520
By keeping the humidity low on the space station, ECLSS provides a comfortable environment for astronauts,

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:11.570
plus keeps things like bacteria or fungus from growing on the station in unwanted places.

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:14.970
Now here's where it comes full circle.

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:22.680
The moisture that is in the air is pulled out, landing on a cold piece of metal that causes it to condensate,

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.600
just like a dehumidifier would work in your house.

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:29.980
The end result is water that is then treated and made ready to be re-

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.480
used, even as drinking water. In fact, ECLSS can recycle just about any water used on the

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:42.360
space station and turn it into drinkable water. Since water makes up about 60% of each of us,

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:45.000
this is a really important feature on the space station.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:51.650
Any wastewater, any water that comes out of humans or produced by humans doing hygiene

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:54.910
like brushing their teeth, washing their hands, shower water, all that.

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:59.600
Monsi and her team developed ECLSS at Marshall Space Flight Center.

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:04.820
in Huntsville, Alabama, in a space that was once supposed to be a module of the space station,

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:08.720
but is now used for testing systems that are earmarked for ISS.

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:12.240
When we're in testing, all the hatches are closed.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:13.240
We bring people in.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:18.880
We inject air that is very clean, extremely clean air, that is devoid of humidity

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:21.200
because we do not want humidity from the outside.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:27.640
What we want to do is collect the metabolic load or the perspiration and the humidity.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.940
All that testing led to this.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.540
system that does the same thing on the space station.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:38.360
And the latest addition to ECLSS on the space station, a new water processor, really ups

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:38.740
the ante.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:40.700
We're really excited.

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:47.490
That marks the first time ever, ever, that any nation has done water recycling, including

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:49.600
urine, with the purpose of drinking.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:54.990
We have done something that has never been done before, which is taking all the waste

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:58.410
waters, all of them, clean them up and return it back to the crew.

00:03:58.410 --> 00:03:59.980
It grates

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.320
reduces the amount of water astronauts need to take from the Earth to the station.

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:10.870
In fact, about 92% of the water that is put out by humans is recovered back into the system.

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:15.970
It's an important component in increasing the crew size on the station from 3 to 6.

00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:22.270
It all adds up to the most productive environment for the people who live and work on the International Space Station.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:26.500
All those things, that's exactly what ECLSS does, all those systems maintain.

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.940
the humans from having to worry about their

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.360
surroundings. We take care of that. They take care of the work and the

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:36.180
experiments and the other things.

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:44.990
You can learn more about EECLIS, the International Space Station, and all of NASA at www.nasa.gov.
